/*
 * **Engineering options** — which blueprints each outfitting module can take, and which
 * experimental effects each of those blueprints offers.
 *
 * Sources: EDSY (github.com/taleden/EDSY) eddb.js — its module-group tables carry the
 *          `blueprints` a group offers and the `expeffects` available on that group, and
 *          mark the individual modules that accept no experimental at all. EDSY is (c)
 *          taleden under a CC BY-NC 4.0 License
 *          (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/). The underlying game logic is
 *          Elite Dangerous data, property of Frontier Developments plc, under Frontier's
 *          media-usage terms: https://forums.frontier.co.uk/threads/510879/
 *
 * **An experimental effect belongs to the blueprint, not to the module.** The game offers
 * the experimental slot inside an applied blueprint: an effect survives a re-roll or a
 * grade increase of the same blueprint and is dropped when the module is switched to a
 * different one. So a list of experimentals is stored against a blueprint, and is only
 * ever read for a (module, blueprint) pair — a blueprint id alone does not identify one,
 * because a Pulse Laser and a Rail Gun both take `Weapon_Efficient` and offer different
 * effects under it.
 *
 * Modules are therefore grouped (`modules` maps a module symbol to a `groups` key) and
 * each group's `blueprints` maps a blueprint id to the experimental-effect ids that
 * blueprint offers on this family. Both key spaces join straight to `blueprints.jsonc`
 * and `experimental-effects.jsonc`.
 *
 * **The per-blueprint lists are an expansion, not a second acquisition.** EDSY publishes
 * one `expeffects` array per module group and coriolis-data one `specials` array beside
 * (not inside) its `blueprints`; neither distinguishes two blueprints of the same group,
 * so every blueprint of a group carries that group's list. The shape is what changed, so
 * that a per-blueprint difference has somewhere to live the moment one is sourced —
 * Anti-Guardian Zone Resistance is the suspected case. See SOURCES.md and
 * https://github.com/DarkSession/Elite-Dangerous-Almanac/issues/33.
 *
 * `exclusions` handles the modules that are the exception to their group: a Multi-cannon
 * cannot take Phasing Sequence, and the mining tools take no experimental at all. Each
 * entry lists the effect ids that module cannot take, already expanded — subtract it from
 * a blueprint's list to get the exact set for that (module, blueprint) pair. Modules
 * absent from `exclusions` take each blueprint's whole list.
 *
 * Only modules the game lets you engineer appear at all; everything else is absent.
 *
 * Full provenance: data/ships/SOURCES.md.
 */
